course: dictionary
lesson: 1546
title: Challenge: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word challenge is a good example. «I challenge you to a race» means invite someone to a contest. A hard task that tests how strong you are is a different meaning, so read the words around it to choose.
explain: «If a lesson is too easy, you can politely ask the teacher for a harder challenge.» The clues are too easy and harder, so challenge means a hard task. Check by swapping in your meaning: a harder hard task still makes sense.
explain: «Checking before you challenge saves you from being wrong in front of everyone.» Here challenge means to question whether something is right or true. The clue is checking: you check before you question. Try the meaning in the sentence and see if it fits.
fact: Challenge can be a noun (a hard task or contest) or a verb (to invite someone to a contest, or to question whether something is right).
fact: «Challenging» means hard, so a challenging puzzle takes real effort to solve.
fact: Phrases such as «take up the challenge» and «rise to the challenge» mean you accept a hard task and try your best.
fact: «A long, hard stare can be a challenge in gorilla language, so keepers ask visitors not to stare at the gorillas.»
fact: Context is the best guide: the words around challenge tell you whether it means a task, a contest or a question.
exercise: «If a lesson is too easy, you can politely ask the teacher for a harder challenge.» What does challenge mean here? | A kind of sweet | A friendly argument with a classmate | *A hard task that tests you | A quiet room for reading
exercise: «I challenge you to a race.» What does challenge mean here? | *To invite someone to a contest | To fall asleep early | To copy a friend's work | To question whether something is true
exercise: «Checking before you challenge saves you from being wrong in front of everyone.» What does challenge mean here? | To win a prize | To hide from the teacher | To run very fast | *To question whether something is right
test: «Rules matter, but some rules are unfair and should be challenged respectfully.» What does challenged mean here? | To give a prize for following the rule | To break every rule on purpose | *To question whether a rule is right, politely | To forget the rule completely
test: Jayden says challenge always means a race. Is he right? | *No, it can mean a hard task, a contest, or questioning whether something is true | No, it is a kind of sweet | Yes, but only at school | Yes, it always means a race
exam: What does «rise to the challenge» mean? | To ask the teacher a question | To stop trying when things get hard | *To accept a hard task and try your best | To run away from a race
